-- title:
--   Linear ODEs $y^{(n)}=\sum_i c_i(z)y^{(i)}+r(z)$ with polynomial coefficients: bounded denominators force a polynomial
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge 0$, let $c_0,\dots,c_{n-1},r\in\mathbb{Q}[z]$ be polynomials, and let $f=\sum_{k\ge 0}a_kz^k\in\mathbb{Q}[[z]]$ be a formal power series satisfying the linear differential equation
--
--   $$f^{(n)}(z)=r(z)+\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}c_i(z)\,f^{(i)}(z).$$
--
--   If there is $N$ such that $a_k\in\mathbb{Z}[1/N]$ for all $k$, then $f$ is a polynomial.
--
--   This proves implication (2)$\Rightarrow$(1) of the Lam–Litt conjecture for the equations $f^{(n)}=g(z,f,\dots,f^{(n-1)})$ in which $g(z,y_0,\dots,y_{n-1})=r(z)+\sum_i c_i(z)\,y_i$ is a polynomial of degree at most one in $y_0,\dots,y_{n-1}$; such an equation has no singular points. Examples are $y'=y$, the Airy equation $y''=zy$, and $y'=m\,z^{m-1}y$ with solution $e^{z^m}$: none of them has a non-polynomial solution in $\mathbb{Q}[[z]]$ with bounded denominators. Linear equations whose leading coefficient is a non-constant polynomial, such as hypergeometric equations, are not covered.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The equation is stated as an identity of formal power series, with the polynomials $c_i$ and $r$ viewed as power series and $f^{(i)}$ the $i$-th formal derivative; for $n=0$ it reads $f=r$. The ring $\mathbb{Z}[1/N]$ is the subalgebra of $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by $1/N$ (for $N=0$ it is $\mathbb{Z}$). The conclusion is that $f$ is the image of a polynomial.
-- source:
--   Special case (linear equations with polynomial coefficients and leading coefficient 1) of implication (2) => (1) of Conjecture 1.1.1 of Y. H. J. Lam, D. Litt, Algebraicity and integrality of solutions to differential equations, https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.13175 (v1), p. 3, which is Problem 1 of D. Litt, https://www.problemsilike.com/1 . Not stated in the paper; deduced from the theorem on entire solutions (LamLitt.polynomial_of_integrality_of_entire on this platform).

import Definitions.Def_LamLitt_Defs
import Mathlib

open PowerSeries

namespace LamLitt

theorem polynomial_of_integrality_of_monic_linear_ode {n : ℕ} (f : PowerSeries ℚ)
    (c : Fin n → Polynomial ℚ) (r : Polynomial ℚ)
    (hODE : (PowerSeries.derivative ℚ)^[n] f =
      (r : PowerSeries ℚ) +
        ∑ i : Fin n, (c i : PowerSeries ℚ) * (PowerSeries.derivative ℚ)^[i.val] f)
    (N : ℕ) (hN : IsCoeffIntegralAdjointInvNat f N) :
    ∃ P : Polynomial ℚ, f = (P : PowerSeries ℚ) := by sorry

end LamLitt
